From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 15:34:46 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Keep font fallback correct while faces load

A family registers one face per delivered source, so mid-load it can
hold only some of its declared faces. CSS matching then picks whatever
did arrive, which for Google Fonts means upright text is shaped with the
italic face: the stylesheet declares all seven italic/400 subsets before
any upright one, so the window is wide. No browser substitutes across
styles like that; during a face's block period the generic fallback is
used and the text re-shapes on arrival.

Track declared-but-undelivered faces as (family, font-style descriptor)
and drop such a family from the parley font stack while a face that
would serve the requested style is still outstanding, so the next family
in the list wins instead. Registration already invalidates inline
contexts, which re-shapes the text with the real face.

Faces reach the document by two routes and both have to be noted, or the
guard silently does nothing for half the web: a <style> or <link> sheet
lands in add_stylesheet_for_node, while an @import'ed sheet is parsed
off the document and arrives as a PendingFontFaces event instead.

Every path that finishes a source has to settle its face or the family
stays blocked for good: the delivery arm clears whether or not
registration succeeds (an unparseable .eot is done loading either way),
and the unsniffable-format arm clears too, since it returns
Resource::None and so never reaches that delivery. That arm catches both
the empty body a failed fetch reports and an HTML error page served 200
under a src with no format() descriptor.

Source selection also recognizes common format() aliases and infers the
format from the URL extension with the query string and fragment
stripped, so a supported source is no longer skipped.
